  "notes": "The song-first traditionalist, and a former bass player, which is why the low end is the most compositional thing here. The key concentration is documented: \"G major the dominant key across the catalogue he is best known for,\" with \"I–V–vi–IV and I–IV–V, and a IV–I plagal loop under the island cuts.\" The room criterion is a real humanize fact rather than a gear one: rooms are chosen for \"playback comfort and space for the whole band in the control room rather than acoustic optimisation,\" i.e. a full-band live capture, so the timing is played and the velocity spread is wide. Drums are \"2&4 with light dry kits, congas/bongos and shaker on the beach cuts, quarter kick and 8th hats on the rockers\" — the conga and bongo lanes are the section's other appearance of hand percussion. The bass carries a stated structural habit: \"root-fifth quarters on the party cuts, long sustains on the ballads, and a documented willingness to have the bass sit out verse 1 entirely.\" He selects microphones purely by ear and delegates all gear specifics, so \"his parameter set is arrangement and casting, not signal chain,\" which is why nothing here is authored from a chain. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the velocity tiers and the counter ratio.",
